//? Title: Filtered Back-Projection: Finite Geometry, Filtering and Reconstruction
//? Subtitle: Native pixel splats, a paired backprojection, exact pipeline stages and measured disk errors
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Recover a square image from projection samples taken at several angles.
Each projection combines information from many pixels, so simply spreading
it back over the image produces blur rather than an inverse. Filtered
back-projection sharpens the projection rows before gathering them onto the
image. We begin with one off-center pixel and its actual detector weights,
then inspect the finite ramp response and retain the original 96 × 96 disk
with 120 views. Native APIs construct the phantom and angles and expose the
actual filter/gather stages. Image, sinogram and profile plots accompany
measured errors, sampling controls and an explicit distinction between an
ideal inversion formula and this finite discrete implementation.
)

\toc

> One pixel deposits into two detector bins

The native image is a row-major N × N array. Its centered coordinates
are x=c−(N−1)/2 and y=r−(N−1)/2 in pixel-spacing units. At angle theta,
a detector coordinate is s=x cos(theta)+y sin(theta)+(D−1)/2. The native
forward operator deposits the pixel value into floor(s) and floor(s)+1
with weights 1−fraction and fraction; bins outside 0..D−1 get nothing.
This is a finite linear pixel splat, rather than exact integration of a
piecewise-constant image along every ray.

For N=3,D=3 and a unit pixel at(row1,column2), x=1,y=0. At theta=pi/4,
s=1+1/sqrt(2), so bins one and two receive approximately0.292893 and
0.707107. Four uniformly spaced angles preserve the unit mass in this tiny
case. The figure uses those actual native outputs, and the table includes
all four views.

The exposed back_projection gathers with the same weights, then multiplies
by pi/M. If A denotes the finite splat matrix, B=(pi/M)A-transpose in
real arithmetic. The dot-product check below tests that paired relationship
on the actual tiny input. A transpose is not an inverse.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/fbp_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
