> The ramp response and the finite filter actually used

In the ideal parallel-beam setting, a projection's one-dimensional Fourier
transform samples a radial line of the image's two-dimensional transform.
Writing Fourier inversion in polar coordinates introduces a frequency
magnitude factor. Filtering each projection by that ramp, then integrating
backprojected filtered samples over a half turn, gives the ideal inversion.
The normalizing constants depend on the frequency convention.

Here use frequency nu in cycles per detector-spacing unit and the inverse
transform exp(2 pi i nu t). Bandlimit the ramp to |nu|≤1/2. Its sampled
impulse response is h[k]=integral from−1/2 to1/2 of |nu|exp(2 pi i nu k)dnu.
Thus h[0]=1/4, h[k]=−1/(pi²k²) for odd k, and zero for nonzero even k.
These are the unit-spacing samples in Kak and Slaney, equation61.

The native ram_lak_filter performs the original direct convolution with
lags −(D−1)..D−1, zero extension and the original summation order. It is
not circular FFT convolution. The impulse response below comes from a
native filtered unit impulse; the other panel filters a finite constant
row. Its nonzero response shows that a finite zero-extended constant row
is not an infinite DC signal annihilated by an ideal ramp.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/fbp_filter.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
